23:28:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s a great question, and you’re absolutely right—72°F can feel quite different depending on the season, even though the thermostat says the same number. The key is that “room temperature” is just the air temperature, but how warm or cool you *feel* depends on several other factors that change with winter conditions. Here are the main reasons: ### 1. Your Body Loses Heat to Cold Surfaces (Radiant Heat Loss) - In winter, exterior walls, windows, and even floors are much colder than the indoor air. - Your body constantly radiates heat to cooler surfaces around you. When those surfaces are cold, you lose more body heat, making you feel chilly even if the air is 72°F. - In summer, those same surfaces are warmer, so you lose less heat and feel warmer at the same air temperature. ### 2. Lower Humidity - Cold air holds less moisture, and heating it up further lowers the *relative* humidity indoors. - Dry air accelerates evaporation of moisture from your skin, which cools you down (just like sweat evaporating). Even if you’re not noticeably sweating, that slight evaporation makes you feel cooler. - More humid summer air slows evaporation, so you feel warmer. ### 3. Drafts and Air Movement - Even in a well-sealed house, small leaks around windows and doors, or convection currents near cold glass, create subtle drafts. - Moving air pulls heat away from your skin faster than still air (the “wind chill” effect). A barely noticeable draft can make 72°F feel like 68°F or lower. ### 4. Your Body’s Seasonal Adaptation - Over weeks and months, your body acclimates. In winter, your skin’s blood flow pattern changes to conserve heat, and you become more sensitive to cold. In summer, your body gets better at shedding heat. - So the same 72°F that felt warm in August might feel cool in February simply because your internal thermostat has shifted. ### 5. Clothing and Activity Level - This one is obvious but tied to behavior. In summer you likely wear lighter, more breathable fabrics, while in winter you bundle up. But at rest, even with a sweater, if your skin is exposed (face, hands), you’ll still notice the radiant and evaporative cooling more. ### In a Nutshell The thermostat is measuring *air* temperature, but your comfort is dictated by the *operative temperature*—a combination of air temperature, radiant temperature, humidity, and airflow. In winter, cold windows and walls, dry air, and tiny drafts team up to lower that effective temperature, making 72°F feel much colder than it does in summer. If you’re trying to feel warmer in winter without cranking up the heat, consider adding a humidifier, sealing drafts, or using radiant heating panels—those address the real reasons you feel cold at exactly the same number.
