ws, and floor are all around 75–80°F, so at 72°F air temperature you feel neutral or warm. In January, those same surfaces might be 60–65°F, so your body radiates more heat to them, making you feel cool at the same 72°F air. The fact that the feeling is consistent house-wide now just confirms your house is uniformly losing heat to the outside. ### What about humidity and drafts? Those also tend to be house-wide. A furnace dries out the air everywhere, and small pressure differences cause a negligible but steady air movement through the whole building envelope. So again, all rooms share the same conditions. ### So, does your observation prove the whole house is "at room temperature"? Yes—if by "room temperature" you mean the thermostat's reading. But your body doesn't have a thermostat; it feels the *operative* temperature. All rooms being equally chilly at 72°F simply means the whole house is operating at a lower radiant temperature, uniformly. That's exactly what we'd expect. In short: your walk-around test doesn't disprove the winter chill phenomenon; it just shows that the phenomenon affects your entire living space equally—which is exactly why you feel it as "72 but cold" rather than "some rooms are drafty."
